WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:04.210
JOHN GABRIELI: A very large
number of children just

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:07.210
aren't having the reading skills
they need to succeed in school.

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.610
The statistics in the US
are that so many children

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:11.680
are behind because of
learning differences,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.870
because of poverty.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.960
The more we understand about
how the mind and brain learns,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.180
the more we might be able
to use that knowledge

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.250
to enhance outcomes
for many learners

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:24.080
at all stages of the lifespan

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.740
One area that I think would
be fantastic to really grow

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.130
is the use of
technology in education.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.130
Technology has revolutionized
practically every aspect

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:35.250
of our lives.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.870
It has not moved education
forward one iota.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.810
There is almost no evidence that
anything we do in technology

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.050
has helped kids read better,
do math better, have better

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.343
social, emotional well-being.

00:00:46.343 --> 00:00:48.260
In fact, we're going the
opposite way on that.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:51.753
Rates of depression and
anxiety are up in adolescence.

00:00:51.753 --> 00:00:53.420
Even before COVID,
in the United States,

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.770
we've had a clear
mental health crisis,

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.060
especially amongst adolescents
and young adults, which

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.393
is so worrying and touching
so many families' lives.

00:01:02.393 --> 00:01:04.310
Some of the research
directions we're pursuing

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.470
is early identification
of risks,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.125
so you don't wait until a child
is in a full-blown depression,

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:11.750
and then you can
hopefully do something

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.700
on behalf of their child.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.920
We know technology can
be amazingly powerful.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.270
It can be highly
personalized to individuals.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:21.230
And so we think that that's
a huge both opportunity

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:25.188
and challenge to say, what
can we do using the best

00:01:25.188 --> 00:01:27.230
that we understand about
modern technology, which

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:29.563
has revolutionized practically
every sphere of our life,

00:01:29.563 --> 00:01:33.450
except educational outcomes
on behalf of children?

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.350
We want the kinds of skills
in technology and science

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:39.050
and engineering that
are so amazing at MIT

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.590
to be aimed at issues of
educational improvement.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:46.790
That kind of system between
scientific knowledge

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.800
and implementation has
not occurred very well

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:49.730
in education.

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.818
We just haven't had
much of a system

00:01:51.818 --> 00:01:54.110
to say, oh, if we know
something about how learning can

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:57.170
be improved, whether
it's learning to read,

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:00.710
STEM learning, social,
emotional well-being on behalf

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:03.560
of children, how do we
know what program works,

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.770
for whom it works, and how
to implement it effectively?

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:10.850
I think that MIT can contribute
to this sphere of opportunity

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:12.050
in social equity--

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.510
is to push not only the
technology and the science,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.590
but also a scientific approach
to saying what really works,

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:19.220
and for whom.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:22.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

